Original commit body from 9c36bf22561a5c2bd1eec2512c37fcb59e723027:

`io:read`, `io:get_line` and `io:get_chars` were declared `logging` -- an
OUTBOUND sink -- for functions that read input. Wrong in both directions at
once: a tainted value "reaching logging" fired at a call that emits nothing, and
the data actually read was no kind of source. elixir inherits erlang, so one
edit clears both.

FOUND ON PURPOSE, NOT BY LUCK, WHICH IS THE POINT. F5 turned up while
attributing an unrelated inert result. This came from a systematic direction
check across all fifteen catalogues: primitives whose NAME is unambiguous about
direction sitting under a boundary of the opposite direction. Twelve hits,
ELEVEN OF THEM CORRECT -- Phoenix's post/put name the HTTP method of a route
they RECEIVE, send_download sends, haskell's readProcess crosses at the process
launch, fetch sends the request, and objc's
NSURLConnection.sendSynchronousRequest: is declared `simultaneous`.

The sweep is now a REGRESSION GUARD in the test file, and it exempts
simultaneous primitives BY PROPERTY rather than by listing the pair -- so the
next honestly-both primitive is exempt automatically while a new
single-direction mistake still fails and has to be adjudicated.

THE FIX IS A RULING, NOT A RE-LABEL. The direction is unambiguous; WHICH inbound
boundary is not. `io:read/1` reads standard input (ipc_recv, following the C
precedent where a bare `stdin` classifies that way); `io:read/2` reads a given
IoDevice, which may be a FILE (fs_read). The catalogue matches on NAME, not
arity -- the same reason `time.localtime` is refused elsewhere in this item --
so exactly one is true per call site and the call site cannot tell which.
Declared under both with `boundary_ruling: call_site_undecidable`, INV-vaduk's
vocabulary and C's `unistd.write` shape. That is a RULING, so the `unruled`
register INV-nular emptied stays empty; asserted.

MEASURED, 2 erlang/elixir repos, per-arm cache, arms sequential, canary on
whether io.read carries an ipc_recv row:

  logging   480 -> 476   (-4)      ejabberd -1, emqx -3
  fs_read   403 -> 407   (+4)
  all 12 other boundaries IDENTICAL

A clean move of four chains out of a false outbound sink.

AND THE HALF THAT DID NOT HAPPEN, stated because a test name claimed it.
`ipc_recv` stayed at ZERO. `classify_call` yields ONE primitive, so an
undecidable pair reports whichever row the lookup finds first, and `fs_read`
won (the INV-zumin row-order hazard, benign here). Since `fs_read` is
deliberately outside AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP, A STDIN READ STILL DOES NOT BECOME A
TAINT SOURCE. That is a recall-side question, band-stopped, and is not achieved
here. The test asserting it was renamed and its claim corrected rather than left
to imply otherwise.

NOTED, NOT CHANGED: the sibling elixir `IO` module rows read/gets/getn as
fs_read only -- the other half of the same ambiguity, picked without a ruling.
Recorded in the erlang row's note rather than changed in passing.

